# Keep the build context small and, more importantly, keep secrets and local state out of
# it. Anything listed here cannot end up in an image layer by accident.

**/bin/
**/obj/
**/.vs/
**/.vscode/
**/.idea/

# Build output written under a literal backslash name.
#
# Some tooling — the Roslyn BuildHost behind an editor's language server, in practice — emits a
# Windows-style relative path on a POSIX filesystem, creating a single directory actually NAMED
# "bin\Debug" rather than the nested bin/Debug above. The `**/bin/` rule does not match it: that
# is a different name.
#
# This is not cosmetic. Left in the context it is COPIED into the build stage, and MSBuild's
# default resource glob then fails enumerating a directory whose name contains a backslash:
#
#   error MSB3552: Resource file "**/*.resx" cannot be found.
#
# — a failure with nothing to do with resources, which appears only in the container build and
# only on a machine that happens to have opened the project in an editor. The same rule exists
# in .gitignore for the same reason.
**/bin\\Debug/
**/bin\\Release/
**/obj\\Debug/
**/obj\\Release/

.git/
.github/
.claude/

tests/
docs/
deploy/
README.md

**/*.user
**/*.suo
**/.DS_Store

# Credentials and local overrides. .env in particular holds the GitHub Packages token —
# keeping it out of the context matters because the Dockerfile now takes that token as a
# build arg, so nothing here should also ship it as a file.
.env
**/.env
**/appsettings.Development.json
**/appsettings.Local.json

Dockerfile*
docker-compose*.yml
